# Thread-Safe Singleton in Modern C++ (C++17)

## 1. Recommended: Meyers' Singleton (simplest and correct since C++11/17)

Since C++11, the standard guarantees that a function-local `static` object is initialized exactly once, even under concurrent entry. This is the idiomatic, zero-boilerplate solution.

```cpp
// SingletonMeyers.hpp
#pragma once

class SingletonMeyers {
 public:
  // Deleted special members to enforce singularity.
  SingletonMeyers(const SingletonMeyers&)            = delete;
  SingletonMeyers& operator=(const SingletonMeyers&) = delete;
  SingletonMeyers(SingletonMeyers&&)                 = delete;
  SingletonMeyers& operator=(SingletonMeyers&&)      = delete;

  // Accessor — initialization is thread-safe per [stmt.dcl]/4.
  static SingletonMeyers& instance() {
    static SingletonMeyers s;   // Initialized once, on first call.
    return s;
  }

  void do_work() const noexcept { /* ... */ }

 private:
  SingletonMeyers()  = default;   // Private: external construction forbidden.
  ~SingletonMeyers() = default;   // Private: external destruction forbidden.

  int state_{0};                  // Example member state.
